  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Any leftover fog from this morning has since\ndissipated, but low clouds still generally remain around the \nCarteret and Craven vicinity this morning. Latest satellite \nimagery shows these low clouds lifting and dissipating as well \nso would expect partly cloudy skies to return once again by mid\nmorning across ENC. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Guidance continues to suggest \ntoday will be primarily dry with only an isolated shower or \nstorm possible on area breezes in the afternoon, but carrying \nless than 15% PoP over land areas. On Wednesday, surface flow \nbecomes more onshore, kicking off a period of increasing \nmoisture and instability across the Southeast U.S. coastline \nwith a modest uptick of shower and thunderstorm coverage. \nAnother wave of low pressure is forecast to lift along the \noffshore front Thursday into Friday, pulling it back towards the\ncoast and providing a better focal point for more widespread \ncoverage of showers and storms ahead of another front to cross \nthe area SAT. High pressure builds back in behind SAT's front on\nSUN.",
